      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100}\\), we start by analyzing the properties of the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). Let's denote \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{I}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix. We have seen that \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{I}\\), where the identity matrix \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) has the property that \\(\\mathbf{I}^n = \\mathbf{I}\\) for any integer \\(n\\). Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{A}^{2k} = \\mathbf{I}\\) for any integer \\(k\\).\n\nSince \\(100 = 2 \\times 50\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{100} = (\\mathbf{A}^2)^{50} = \\mathbf{I}^{50} = \\mathbf{I}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
